.TH CHMAP_DELETE_ELEM 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_delete_elem \- remove a key-value pair from the hash map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

ccol_retval_t chmap_delete_elem(chmap chm, const cmap_pair *key_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Removes the key-value pair with the specified key from the hash map and
frees the memory of both the key and the value. The map shrinks
automatically when its load factor falls below a threshold.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to remove from
key_pair   Key to remove
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        - Key removed successfully
ccol_invalid_args   - chm or key_pair is NULL or key size is 0; also
                      returned when key_pair->size does not exactly
                      match the byte size of the key type, for any
                      fixed-width numeric key type (char, short, int,
                      long, long long, their unsigned counterparts,
                      float, double, long double, or a pointer type),
                      regardless of which backend the map uses
ccol_key_not_found  - Key does not exist in map
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair needs no specified alignment, because the map
hashes and compares the key bytes through memcpy instead of through a
pointer cast to the key type. key_pair->ptr can therefore point directly
at key bytes at any offset in a larger buffer, such as a field in a
packed network frame or a memory-mapped record, and you do not have to
copy the key bytes into an aligned local variable first
Average O(1) complexity
Frees the memory of the key and of the value
Open addressing: shrinks when the load factor < 0.25 (scale 0.5x). A
grow on insert always leaves the live load above 0.25, so a delete
never shrinks straight back a table that an insert just grew
Open addressing: the delete moves the later entries of the same
cluster back into the gap (backward-shift deletion) and leaves no
marker for a deleted slot. Entries of that cluster can change slot, so
every reference that chmap_get_elem_ref(3) or chmap_get_ptr(3) gave
is invalid after the delete, as after any change to the map
Separate chaining: shrinks when elem_count < (bucket_count + 1) / 8 (scale 0.25x)
For a float or double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's
key equality contract (bitwise, except -0.0 canonicalized to 0.0)
For a long double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's key
equality contract (value-based, unlike every other key type)
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_remove (3),
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3)
